export const DATA_59: ReadingTest[] = [
  { id: "rd-077", num: 77, title: "Đề 077", timeMin: 60, parts: [
    { title: "Air Pollution in Modern Cities", paragraphs: ["Air pollution is one of the most serious environmental health problems facing cities around the world today. In many large urban areas, the quality of the air that people breathe every day has reached levels that doctors and scientists consider genuinely dangerous. According to the World Health Organization, approximately seven million people die each year as a result of air pollution — more than from any other single environmental cause. Despite this alarming figure, pollution levels in many cities continue to rise, and millions of residents go about their daily lives largely unaware of the invisible risks surrounding them.", "The main sources of urban air pollution vary from city to city, but motor vehicles are consistently among the most significant contributors worldwide. Cars, buses, and trucks burn fossil fuels that release a mixture of harmful substances into the atmosphere, including nitrogen dioxide, carbon monoxide, and fine particulate matter — tiny particles so small that they can pass through the lining of the lungs and enter the bloodstream directly. In cities where public transport systems are underdeveloped and car ownership is high, traffic-related pollution can be responsible for the majority of total air pollution in urban areas. Industrial activity, power generation, and the burning of waste also contribute significantly to the overall problem.", "The health effects of long-term exposure to polluted air are wide-ranging and severe. Children are among the most vulnerable members of the population, as their lungs are still developing and they breathe in more air relative to their body weight than adults do. Studies have shown that children growing up in highly polluted cities are more likely to develop asthma, suffer from reduced lung capacity, and perform less well academically than those raised in areas with cleaner air. Elderly people and those with existing heart or respiratory conditions are similarly at risk. For the general population, long-term exposure to high levels of air pollution has been strongly linked to heart disease, stroke, lung cancer, and premature death.", "Some cities have taken bold steps to reduce pollution levels, with encouraging results. In 2003, London introduced a congestion charge — a daily fee for driving a private vehicle in the city center — that led to a measurable reduction in traffic and associated emissions. Oslo, the capital of Norway, has gone even further, offering generous financial incentives for the purchase of electric vehicles and investing heavily in charging infrastructure, with the result that electric cars now account for a majority of new vehicle sales in the country. Several Chinese cities, including Beijing, have introduced strict limits on the number of new vehicle registrations permitted each year, aiming to cap the total number of cars on the road.", "Green spaces within cities also play an important role in improving air quality. Trees and other plants absorb carbon dioxide and release oxygen, but they also help to trap fine particulate matter on their leaves and bark, reducing the concentration of harmful particles in the air nearby. Research conducted in several European cities found that streets lined with trees had significantly lower levels of particulate pollution than comparable streets without vegetation. Urban planners are increasingly incorporating green corridors — strips of vegetation linking parks and green areas across a city — into their designs as a low-cost and aesthetically pleasing way to improve air quality in dense urban neighborhoods.", "Individual behavior also matters. Choosing to walk, cycle, or use public transport instead of driving a private car significantly reduces each person's contribution to urban air pollution. Avoiding outdoor exercise during periods of high pollution — which are often indicated by air quality index readings available through smartphone applications — can reduce a person's exposure to harmful particles on the worst days. Ventilating homes carefully, avoiding the indoor burning of candles or incense, and using air purifiers in heavily polluted neighborhoods are among the practical steps that residents can take to protect themselves and their families from the worst effects of polluted urban air."], questions: [{ q: "What is the passage mainly about?", options: ["The causes, effects, and possible solutions to air pollution in cities", "How children are more seriously affected by air pollution than any other group", "Why electric vehicles are the most important solution to urban pollution", "The history of air pollution regulations in Europe and their effectiveness"], answer: 0 }, { q: "According to the passage, how many people die each year as a result of air pollution?", options: ["Nearly five million", "Approximately seven million", "More than ten million", "Around one million"], answer: 1 }, { q: "According to the passage, what measure did London introduce in 2003 to reduce traffic pollution?", options: ["Financial rewards for residents who gave up their cars permanently", "A requirement that all buses and taxis switch to electric power", "A daily fee for driving a private car in central areas of the city", "A ban on all diesel-powered vehicles entering the city center"], answer: 2 }, { q: "According to the passage, how do trees help to reduce air pollution in cities?", options: ["By producing chemicals that break down nitrogen dioxide in the atmosphere", "By catching fine particles on their leaves and bark, lowering the concentration of harmful matter nearby", "By cooling the air around them, which causes pollutants to fall to the ground rather than spreading", "By blocking wind that would otherwise carry pollutants from industrial areas into neighborhoods"], answer: 1 }, { q: "According to the passage, what approach has Oslo taken to reduce vehicle pollution?", options: ["Introducing a congestion charge for all vehicles entering the city center", "Setting an annual limit on the total number of new vehicles that can be registered", "Providing strong financial incentives for buying electric cars and building charging infrastructure", "Banning the registration of new petrol and diesel cars within the city limits"], answer: 2 }, { q: "The word \"those\" in paragraph 3 refers to _______.", options: ["elderly people and those with existing heart or respiratory conditions", "children who have grown up in places with better air quality", "children who have developed asthma as a result of living in polluted areas", "researchers who conducted studies on lung development in urban children"], answer: 1 }, { q: "According to the passage, which of the following is NOT mentioned as a step individuals can take to reduce their exposure to air pollution?", options: ["Choosing to walk, cycle, or use public transport rather than driving", "Using an air purifier inside the home in polluted neighborhoods", "Checking air quality index readings before exercising outdoors", "Writing to local government officials to demand stricter pollution laws"], answer: 3 }, { q: "The word \"vulnerable\" in paragraph 3 is closest in meaning to _______.", options: ["more likely to recover quickly after a health problem", "physically weaker than average for their age group", "easily harmed or at greater risk of negative effects", "unaware of the dangers present in their environment"], answer: 2 }, { q: "The word \"corridors\" in paragraph 5 is closest in meaning to _______.", options: ["areas of land set aside for the protection of endangered plant species", "strips of connected green space linking parks across a city", "designated lanes reserved for cyclists in busy urban streets", "enclosed indoor passageways connecting different parts of a building"], answer: 1 }, { q: "What is the author's overall attitude toward the problem of air pollution in cities?", options: ["Concerned but constructive, highlighting the seriousness of the problem while pointing to effective solutions at different levels", "Pessimistic, arguing that pollution levels are now too high for any intervention to make a meaningful difference", "Uncritical of governments, suggesting that city authorities are already doing enough to protect residents", "Neutral and purely informational, presenting facts without suggesting that action is needed"], answer: 0 }] },
    { title: "Extreme Weather Around the World", paragraphs: ["In recent years, extreme weather events have become more frequent, more intense, and more geographically widespread than at any point in recorded meteorological history. Prolonged heatwaves, catastrophic flooding, devastating wildfires, and unusually powerful storm systems have affected communities on every inhabited continent, leaving scientists, policymakers, and ordinary citizens struggling to understand and respond to a pattern of disruption that is increasingly difficult to dismiss as random variation. The emerging scientific consensus is unambiguous: climate change, driven primarily by the accumulation of greenhouse gases in the atmosphere from human activity, is the dominant force amplifying the frequency and severity of extreme weather worldwide.", "Heatwaves represent one of the deadliest and most underestimated forms of extreme weather. Unlike the dramatic visual impact of flooding or wildfire, heat kills its victims quietly, through dehydration, heat stroke, and the exacerbation of existing cardiovascular and respiratory conditions. The European heatwave of summer 2003 claimed an estimated 70,000 lives across the continent — a toll comparable to a major natural disaster — yet it attracted far less sustained public attention than events of similar scale involving more visible destruction. As global average temperatures rise, the frequency and duration of heatwaves are projected to increase sharply, with regions that currently experience extreme heat only rarely expected to face it as a routine seasonal occurrence within decades.", "Flooding has affected an extraordinary range of communities in recent years, from low-lying Pacific island nations — where even modest rises in sea level combine with storm surges to inundate villages — to major European cities including London and Paris, which experienced unprecedented flooding events in 2021. The causes are multiple and interacting: warmer air holds more moisture, intensifying rainfall during storm events; rising sea levels increase the reach of coastal storm surges; and the replacement of permeable natural surfaces with impermeable urban infrastructure reduces the capacity of land to absorb water rapidly. [1] Communities at greatest risk are typically those with the least financial capacity to invest in flood defenses, early warning systems, and post-disaster recovery — a pattern that concentrates vulnerability disproportionately among lower-income populations in developing nations.", "Wildfire has emerged as one of the most globally visible consequences of hotter and drier conditions. The 2019–2020 Australian bushfire season — known informally as the \"Black Summer\" — burned approximately 18.6 million hectares, destroyed over 3,000 homes, killed an estimated one billion animals, and generated smoke plumes that affected air quality as far away as South America. In the western United States, the annual area burned by wildfires has more than doubled since the 1980s, and the fire season has extended by an average of 78 days compared to historical norms. Climate scientists note that while wildfires have always been a natural part of many ecosystems, the combination of prolonged drought, record temperatures, and expanded human settlement in fire-prone areas has transformed them from manageable natural events into civilization-scale crises. [2]", "Tropical storms and hurricanes have also drawn intense scientific attention. While the total number of tropical storms has not increased dramatically, research suggests that a higher proportion of those that do form are reaching the highest intensity categories — Category 4 and 5 on the Saffir-Simpson scale — than was typical in previous decades. Warmer ocean surface temperatures provide more energy for storm intensification, and slower-moving storms — a trend documented in several ocean basins — dump more rainfall in a single location, dramatically amplifying flood damage. Hurricane Harvey in 2017 stalled over the Houston metropolitan area for several days, depositing more than 60 inches of rain and causing an estimated 125 billion dollars in damage, making it one of the most expensive weather disasters in United States history. [3]", "The human and economic costs of extreme weather are staggering and rising. A report published by the United Nations Office for Disaster Risk Reduction found that weather-related disasters caused losses of more than 2.9 trillion dollars globally between 2000 and 2019 — nearly double the total from the preceding twenty years. Developing countries bear a disproportionate share of these losses relative to their economic size, in large part because they lack the infrastructure, early warning systems, and financial reserves needed to absorb and recover from repeated shocks. The concept of \"loss and damage\" — the irreversible harm caused by climate change impacts that cannot be prevented by adaptation measures alone — has become one of the most contentious issues in international climate negotiations, with vulnerable nations demanding compensation from historically high-emitting countries. [4]", "Adaptation and mitigation represent the two complementary strategic responses to extreme weather. Mitigation — reducing the greenhouse gas emissions that drive climate change — is ultimately the only way to limit the long-term intensification of extreme events, and the commitments made under the Paris Agreement, if fully implemented, would substantially reduce but not eliminate the projected increase in extreme weather frequency and severity. Adaptation — adjusting infrastructure, land use planning, early warning systems, and social safety nets to manage the risks that cannot be avoided — is essential for protecting communities in the near term. Neither strategy can substitute for the other, and the evidence increasingly suggests that the window for limiting the most severe outcomes is narrowing faster than the pace of either global mitigation effort or adaptation investment would justify."], questions: [{ q: "What is the main purpose of this passage?", options: ["To argue that governments have failed to take any meaningful action against climate change", "To examine the growing frequency and impact of extreme weather events and the responses available to address them", "To compare the economic damage caused by different types of extreme weather around the world", "To explain why developing nations suffer more from extreme weather than wealthier countries"], answer: 1 }, { q: "According to the passage, approximately how many people died during the European heatwave of 2003?", options: ["Nearly 700,000", "More than one million", "Approximately 70,000", "Around 7,000"], answer: 2 }, { q: "According to the passage, what was one consequence of Australia's \"Black Summer\" bushfire season?", options: ["It displaced over three million people and required international humanitarian assistance.", "It permanently destroyed all forest cover across southeastern Australia.", "It killed an estimated one billion animals and spread smoke as far as South America.", "It caused the government to introduce a nationwide ban on land clearing."], answer: 2 }, { q: "According to the passage, how much did weather-related disasters cost globally between 2000 and 2019?", options: ["Around 2.9 trillion dollars", "More than 4 trillion dollars", "Approximately 1.5 trillion dollars", "Nearly 2 trillion dollars"], answer: 0 }, { q: "According to the passage, why was Hurricane Harvey particularly damaging in terms of rainfall?", options: ["It struck the Houston area at the highest wind speed ever recorded for a Category 5 storm.", "It formed more rapidly than any previous hurricane recorded in the Gulf of Mexico.", "It stalled over Houston for several days, depositing over 60 inches of rain in one location.", "It occurred during an unusually dry period, making the ground unable to absorb any of the rainfall."], answer: 2 }, { q: "According to the passage, which of the following is NOT mentioned as a cause of increased flooding?", options: ["The replacement of natural ground cover with hard urban surfaces", "The deliberate removal of flood barriers by governments prioritizing development", "Rising sea levels extending the range of coastal storm surges", "Warmer air retaining greater amounts of moisture during storm events"], answer: 1 }, { q: "The word \"them\" in paragraph 4 refers to _______.", options: ["climate scientists who study the relationship between drought and fire behavior", "the ecosystems in which wildfires have always been a natural occurrence", "wildfires, which climate change has transformed from manageable events into major crises", "human settlements that have expanded into areas prone to wildfire"], answer: 2 }, { q: "The word \"exacerbation\" in paragraph 2 is closest in meaning to _______.", options: ["the worsening of an existing problem or condition", "the sudden onset of a medical condition that had previously shown no symptoms", "a temporary improvement followed by a more serious deterioration", "the transfer of a disease from one person to another in crowded conditions"], answer: 0 }, { q: "The word \"mitigation\" in paragraph 7 is closest in meaning to _______.", options: ["the international legal process by which countries negotiate climate targets", "actions taken to reduce or limit the severity of a harmful process or outcome", "the financial compensation paid to communities damaged by extreme weather events", "a strategy of preparing communities to cope with the effects of climate change"], answer: 1 }, { q: "What can be inferred from the final paragraph about the current pace of global action on climate change?", options: ["Current efforts to reduce emissions and adapt to climate impacts are not advancing quickly enough to prevent the most serious outcomes.", "The Paris Agreement commitments, if implemented, will completely prevent any further increase in extreme weather.", "Adaptation measures alone are sufficient to protect most communities from the effects of extreme weather.", "Developed countries have already done enough to reduce their emissions and should focus resources on helping developing nations adapt."], answer: 0 }] },
    { title: "The Rise of E-Education", paragraphs: ["The transformation of education through digital technology — a process commonly referred to as e-education or e-learning — has reshaped how knowledge is created, shared, and accessed across the world. What was once a marginal supplement to classroom instruction has become, for many learners, the primary mode of education. Driven by the proliferation of internet connectivity, the development of sophisticated learning management platforms, and the democratizing potential of open educational resources, e-education now reaches populations that traditional schooling structures were never designed to serve. The pace of this shift, already rapid before 2020, was dramatically accelerated by the COVID-19 pandemic, which forced an abrupt global experiment in remote learning that permanently altered institutional attitudes toward online delivery.", "The range of e-education formats is broad. At one end of the spectrum, asynchronous learning platforms — where students access recorded lectures, readings, and assessments at times of their own choosing — offer maximum scheduling flexibility and are particularly well-suited to working adults managing competing demands. At the other end, synchronous virtual classrooms — where students and instructors interact in real time through video conferencing — seek to replicate the immediacy and responsiveness of in-person instruction. Between these two poles lie blended models that combine online and face-to-face elements in varying proportions, and massive open online courses (MOOCs) that enroll thousands of learners simultaneously in structured but minimally supervised programs. [1]", "Evidence on the effectiveness of e-education is substantial, if mixed. Meta-analyses drawing on hundreds of comparative studies have consistently found that well-designed online instruction produces learning outcomes comparable to, and in some domains superior to, conventional classroom teaching. The advantages are most pronounced in settings that allow students to work at their own pace, revisit recorded content as needed, and receive immediate automated feedback on practice exercises. However, these gains are contingent on factors that are far from universally present: reliable internet access, capable hardware, learner digital literacy, and the availability of instructors with sufficient training to design and facilitate high-quality online learning experiences. Where these conditions are absent, the gap between the potential and the reality of e-education can be wide.", "One of the most significant contributions of e-education has been its potential to expand access for learners who face barriers to conventional schooling. People with physical disabilities, those living in geographically remote communities, adults returning to education after extended breaks, and individuals in low-income countries with limited formal school infrastructure have all, in principle, benefited from e-learning's reduced dependence on physical proximity. [2] Open educational resources — freely available digital materials licensed for reuse and adaptation — have allowed educators in under-resourced settings to access high-quality content that would have been financially inaccessible in print form. Initiatives such as the Khan Academy, MIT OpenCourseWare, and the African Virtual University have demonstrated that the model is operationally viable even in contexts with significant infrastructure constraints.", "Quality assurance presents a persistent challenge in e-education. The ease of publishing online content has led to a proliferation of courses and credentials of highly variable quality, and learners — particularly those unfamiliar with a field — often lack the expertise to evaluate what they encounter. Credential recognition remains uneven: many employers and institutions continue to regard online qualifications with skepticism, even when the substantive content of a program is comparable to its campus equivalent. [3] Accreditation bodies in most countries have been slow to develop coherent frameworks for evaluating online programs, and the rapid growth of for-profit online education providers has introduced incentive structures that do not always align with genuine learning outcomes.", "Pedagogical innovation within e-education has produced tools and methods with significant promise. Adaptive learning systems that monitor individual learner progress and adjust the difficulty and focus of content in real time have shown measurable improvements in retention and engagement in controlled studies. Gamification — the application of game-design principles such as points, badges, and progress bars to learning environments — has demonstrated effectiveness in maintaining motivation among younger learners and in corporate training contexts. Learning analytics platforms that give instructors detailed visibility into student engagement, progress, and likely dropout risk enable early intervention that can meaningfully improve completion rates. [4]", "The future of e-education will be shaped by the resolution of tensions that remain unresolved at its current stage of development. The ambition to provide universal access to quality learning must be reconciled with the reality that access to the digital infrastructure e-education depends upon remains profoundly unequal. The promise of personalized, self-directed learning must be balanced against the evidence that most learners — particularly younger students and those from disadvantaged backgrounds — benefit substantially from structured social interaction, mentorship, and accountability that digital formats struggle to replicate at scale. Meeting these challenges will require not only technological innovation but a serious and sustained commitment from governments, institutions, and civil society to treat equitable access to quality education as a public good rather than a market commodity."], questions: [{ q: "What is the central argument of the passage about e-education?", options: ["The main benefit of e-education is that it reduces the cost of education for governments and institutions.", "MOOCs are the most effective form of e-education because they can reach the largest number of learners simultaneously.", "Despite significant promise in expanding access and improving outcomes, e-education faces unresolved challenges around quality, equity, and the limits of digital interaction.", "E-education has already replaced conventional schooling as the dominant form of education worldwide."], answer: 2 }, { q: "According to the passage, why are asynchronous learning platforms particularly suited to working adults?", options: ["They cost significantly less to develop and maintain than live online classroom systems.", "They allow learners to access content on their own schedule without fixed attendance times.", "They offer stronger peer interaction and collaboration tools than other e-learning formats.", "They provide more detailed instructor feedback than synchronous formats can offer."], answer: 1 }, { q: "According to the passage, which of the following groups is mentioned as having benefited from e-education's reduced dependence on physical proximity?", options: ["Students in wealthy countries who prefer not to commute to campus daily", "Secondary school students preparing for nationally standardized examinations", "Adults returning to education after a long break from formal learning", "University professors seeking to expand their research beyond their home institution"], answer: 2 }, { q: "According to the passage, what have adaptive learning systems been shown to improve?", options: ["The speed at which instructors can grade and return assessments to students", "The ability of learners to collaborate effectively with peers in different time zones", "Levels of knowledge retention and learner engagement in controlled research settings", "The rate at which new course content can be developed and published online"], answer: 2 }, { q: "According to the passage, what problem do many employers and institutions have with online qualifications?", options: ["Employers are concerned that online learners have not developed the technical skills required for professional roles.", "Online programs are rarely accredited and therefore have no legal standing in most countries.", "Online qualifications are accepted only in technology-related industries and not in other professional fields.", "They continue to view online credentials with doubt, even when the program's academic content is equivalent to campus-based study."], answer: 3 }, { q: "According to the passage, which of the following is NOT mentioned as a pedagogical innovation used in e-education?", options: ["Systems that adjust content difficulty based on each learner's individual progress", "Platforms that give teachers data on student engagement and predict dropout risk", "The use of game-design features such as points and progress indicators to maintain motivation", "Virtual reality environments that simulate laboratory experiments and fieldwork experiences"], answer: 3 }, { q: "The word \"these\" in paragraph 2 refers to _______.", options: ["the learning management platforms that support different types of online delivery", "the instructors and students who participate in real-time virtual classrooms", "the competing demands that working adults must manage alongside their studies", "the two ends of the e-education spectrum — asynchronous and synchronous formats"], answer: 3 }, { q: "The word \"contingent\" in paragraph 3 is closest in meaning to _______.", options: ["dependent on particular conditions being present", "limited to a specific period before losing their effect", "significant enough to justify the investment required", "consistent across different learner populations and contexts"], answer: 0 }, { q: "The word \"proliferation\" in paragraph 5 is closest in meaning to _______.", options: ["a gradual improvement in the standard or reliability of a system", "a sudden decline in the availability of a previously common resource", "a formal process of reviewing and approving new products or services", "a rapid increase in the number or variety of something"], answer: 3 }, { q: "Which of the following square brackets [1] , [2] , [3] , or [4] best indicates where the following sentence could be added? 'Each format carries its own trade-offs between flexibility, interactivity, and the depth of the learning community it can sustain.'", options: ["[1]", "[2]", "[3]", "[4]"], answer: 0 }] },
    { title: "The Global Problem of E-Waste", paragraphs: ["Electronic waste — the discarded residue of the consumer electronics industry — has become one of the fastest-growing and most complex solid waste streams in the world. Defined by the United Nations as any device with a plug, battery, or electric circuit that has been discarded rather than reused, the category of e-waste encompasses everything from mobile phones and laptops to refrigerators, medical equipment, and industrial machinery. Global volumes of e-waste reached 62 million metric tons in 2022, according to the Global E-Waste Monitor — a figure representing a 82 percent increase from 2010 — and projections suggest that annual volumes could exceed 82 million metric tons by 2030 if current consumption and disposal patterns continue unchanged. [1] Only about 22 percent of e-waste generated globally is formally documented as collected and recycled; the remainder is dumped, burned, or processed through informal channels with little or no environmental or health protection.", "The composition of e-waste is simultaneously a source of significant economic value and severe environmental risk. Consumer electronics contain recoverable quantities of gold, silver, copper, palladium, and rare earth elements — collectively worth an estimated 91 billion dollars in the 2022 waste stream alone. Recovering these materials through formal recycling would reduce the need for primary mining, lower the carbon footprint of electronics manufacturing, and generate employment in the circular economy. At the same time, the same devices contain hazardous substances — lead, mercury, cadmium, hexavalent chromium, brominated flame retardants — that, when released into the environment through improper disposal, bioaccumulate in food chains and cause serious neurological, developmental, and carcinogenic harm to exposed populations.", "The geography of e-waste flows reflects deep structural inequalities in the global economy. Wealthier nations generate the majority of e-waste but have, historically, exported substantial volumes to developing countries — particularly in West Africa, South and Southeast Asia, and parts of Latin America — under the cover of second-hand goods trade or charitable donation. Once in destination countries, devices deemed non-functional are processed by informal workers — often children — using techniques including open burning of cables to recover copper and immersion in acid baths to extract precious metals. [2] Sites in Agbogbloshie in Ghana and Guiyu in China became internationally notorious as centers of informal e-waste processing, their soil, water, and air contaminated by concentrations of toxic compounds far exceeding safe thresholds by orders of magnitude.", "International and domestic regulatory frameworks have attempted to address these flows with limited success. The Basel Convention, adopted in 1989 and amended in 2019 to more explicitly restrict the export of hazardous e-waste from wealthy to developing countries, provides the primary legal architecture for international e-waste governance. However, enforcement has been inconsistent, definitional ambiguities around what constitutes \"hazardous\" e-waste have created loopholes, and the capacity of many destination countries to monitor imports and prosecute violators remains weak. [3] At the domestic level, extended producer responsibility (EPR) legislation — which places legal and financial responsibility for end-of-life management of electronics on manufacturers — has been implemented in the European Union, Japan, and a growing number of middle-income countries, but global coverage remains partial and enforcement quality varies considerably. [4]", "The most promising responses to the e-waste crisis are those that intervene at multiple points in the electronics lifecycle rather than focusing exclusively on end-of-life management. At the design stage, the principles of the circular economy — designing products for longevity, repairability, and disassembly — can dramatically reduce the volume and toxicity of waste generated. Right-to-repair legislation, already enacted in several jurisdictions, empowers consumers and independent technicians to repair devices rather than replacing them, extending functional lifespans and reducing premature disposal. At the collection and processing stage, investment in formal high-value recycling infrastructure — capable of safely recovering a wider range of materials than informal operations — generates both environmental benefits and economic returns that can be reinvested in system improvement.", "Consumer behavior represents a further lever of change that is both underutilized and culturally variable in its responsiveness to intervention. Research on consumption patterns in high-income countries has found that upgrade cycles for consumer electronics — particularly smartphones — are driven less by functional obsolescence than by marketing, social signaling, and the deliberate design of planned obsolescence features. Campaigns promoting device longevity, second-hand markets, and the normalization of repair culture have shown modest but real effects in some contexts, though their reach is typically limited to consumers who are already environmentally motivated. Structural interventions — including taxes on new device purchases, subsidies for repair services, and mandatory minimum warranty periods — offer more robust and universally applied incentives for behavior change than voluntary campaigns alone.", "The e-waste crisis is ultimately a symptom of a linear economic model in which the extraction of natural resources, the manufacture of short-lived products, and their disposal are treated as externalities to be borne by environments and communities with the least power to resist. Transitioning toward an economy in which materials are kept in use as long as possible — through design, policy, and cultural change — is not merely an environmental aspiration but an economic and social imperative. The scale of the resources wasted in current disposal practices, and the human cost borne by those who process e-waste in unsafe conditions, together constitute a moral case for reform that is, in the view of most researchers and policymakers who engage seriously with the evidence, effectively unanswerable."], questions: [{ q: "What is the central argument of the passage about e-waste?", options: ["Consumer behavior is the primary driver of e-waste and can only be changed through large-scale public awareness campaigns.", "Extended producer responsibility legislation has already resolved the most serious aspects of the e-waste problem in participating countries.", "The Basel Convention is the single most effective tool for solving the global e-waste crisis.", "E-waste is a growing global crisis rooted in a linear economic model, and addressing it requires intervention across the entire electronics lifecycle."], answer: 3 }, { q: "What is the author's attitude toward the case for reforming the global e-waste system?", options: ["Neutral and balanced, presenting arguments for and against reform with equal weight", "Strongly committed, arguing that the moral, economic, and environmental evidence for reform is effectively beyond dispute", "Cautiously optimistic but ultimately uncertain, suggesting that the evidence is still too limited to draw firm conclusions", "Doubtful, noting that the economic costs of reform are likely to outweigh the environmental benefits"], answer: 1 }, { q: "According to the passage, what was the estimated economic value of recoverable materials in the global e-waste stream in 2022?", options: ["Approximately 22 billion dollars", "Around 62 billion dollars", "More than 120 billion dollars", "Roughly 91 billion dollars"], answer: 3 }, { q: "According to the passage, what made sites such as Agbogbloshie in Ghana and Guiyu in China internationally significant?", options: ["They were established as model formal recycling centers demonstrating best practice in e-waste processing.", "They became well known as locations where toxic contamination from informal e-waste processing reached extreme levels.", "They were the sites of major legal cases that led directly to amendments in the Basel Convention.", "They attracted international investment in formal recycling infrastructure after their problems were publicized."], answer: 1 }, { q: "According to the passage, why has the Basel Convention had limited success in controlling e-waste exports?", options: ["Enforcement is inconsistent, definitional loopholes exist, and many destination countries lack the capacity to monitor imports effectively.", "The convention was not ratified by the major e-waste generating nations and therefore has no legal authority over them.", "It focuses only on radioactive waste and does not cover the types of electronic devices that cause the most harm.", "The convention expired in 2019 and has not been renewed due to disagreements between wealthy and developing nations."], answer: 0 }, { q: "According to the passage, what does right-to-repair legislation aim to achieve?", options: ["It requires manufacturers to provide free repairs for all products within a minimum warranty period.", "It establishes government-funded repair centers in low-income communities to reduce the cost of maintaining electronic devices.", "It mandates that all electronic products be designed so that their components can be fully recycled at end of life.", "It gives consumers and independent technicians the ability to repair devices rather than replacing them, extending their usable life."], answer: 3 }, { q: "According to the passage, which of the following is NOT mentioned as a structural policy intervention for changing consumer behavior toward electronics?", options: ["Financial support for repair services to make them more affordable", "Mandatory recycling quotas imposed on consumers based on the number of devices they own", "Taxes applied to the purchase of new electronic devices", "Legal requirements for manufacturers to provide minimum warranty periods"], answer: 1 }, { q: "The word \"these\" in paragraph 2 refers to _______.", options: ["the hazardous substances such as lead, mercury, and cadmium found in electronic devices", "the environmental risks associated with improper disposal of consumer electronics", "the recoverable valuable materials including gold, silver, copper, and rare earth elements", "the developing countries that receive exported e-waste from wealthier nations"], answer: 2 }, { q: "The word \"robust\" in paragraph 6 is closest in meaning to _______.", options: ["widely publicized and supported by environmental advocacy organizations", "strong, reliable, and difficult to circumvent or ignore", "complex and requiring specialist expertise to implement effectively", "gradually introduced so as to minimize disruption to existing industries"], answer: 1 }, { q: "Which of the following square brackets [1] , [2] , [3] , or [4] best indicates where the following sentence could be added? 'The health consequences for these workers — elevated rates of cancer, respiratory disease, and developmental disorders in children — have been extensively documented by researchers from multiple institutions.'", options: ["[1]", "[2]", "[3]", "[4]"], answer: 1 }] },
  ] },
  { id: "rd-083", num: 83, title: "Đề 083", timeMin: 60, parts: [
    { title: "Different Hobbies of Five Friends", paragraphs: ["Minh, Lan, Tuan, Huong, and Bao have been close friends since primary school. Although they all grew up in the same neighborhood in Ho Chi Minh City and attend the same university, each of them has developed a very different hobby that reflects their personality and the way they like to spend their free time. Their friends and families often joke that the five of them represent five completely different worlds, yet their friendships remain as strong as ever.", "Minh is passionate about photography. He spends most of his weekends exploring the streets of Ho Chi Minh City with his camera, capturing images of everyday life — market vendors, children playing in alleyways, and the changing light on old buildings in the morning and evening hours. He saves money carefully each month so that he can buy new lenses and accessories for his camera. Minh has entered several photography competitions organized by the city's cultural centers, and last year he won second place in a category for street photography. He dreams of one day publishing a photography book that documents the people and places of his hometown.", "Lan's hobby is reading, and she approaches it with the same seriousness that others might apply to an academic subject. She keeps a detailed journal in which she records her thoughts about every book she finishes, and she participates in an online reading club where members discuss their latest reads each week. Her personal library at home contains over three hundred books, organized carefully by genre and author. She particularly enjoys historical fiction and has read widely in both Vietnamese and translated foreign literature. When her friends ask her for book recommendations, she always has a long list ready and becomes genuinely excited explaining why each book is worth reading.", "Tuan has a deep love for cooking, which he discovered during the COVID-19 pandemic when he spent several months at home and began experimenting with recipes from different countries. What started as a way to pass the time quickly became a serious pursuit . He now follows cooking channels on YouTube, collects cookbooks from second-hand bookshops, and hosts a small dinner party at his apartment for his four friends at least once a month. Each dinner has a theme — one month might be Japanese cuisine, the next Thai or Italian. His friends all agree that his cooking has improved remarkably over the past two years, and Bao in particular is always the first to arrive and the last to leave.", "Huong is the most physically active of the group. She began running three years ago when her doctor advised her to exercise more regularly after a period of poor health, and she quickly discovered that she genuinely loved the sport. She now runs five days a week, typically in the early morning before her university classes begin, and has completed two half-marathons in the past year. She tracks her progress carefully using a fitness app on her phone and follows a structured training program that she found online. Her goal for the coming year is to complete a full marathon, and she has already registered for a race scheduled to take place in Da Nang in November.", "Bao's hobby is board gaming. He owns a collection of over fifty board games, ranging from simple party games that can be played in thirty minutes to complex strategy games that require several hours to complete. He organizes a board game night every two weeks, usually on a Friday or Saturday evening, and the five friends often use these gatherings as an opportunity to catch up with one another. Bao is also a member of a board game club at the university, where he has met many new friends who share his interest. His favorite game at the moment is a strategy title called Terraforming Mars , which he says requires careful planning and cooperation between players to succeed. He has introduced the game to all four of his close friends, though only Tuan has become as enthusiastic about it as he is."], questions: [{ q: "What is the main topic of the passage?", options: ["How five young people manage to balance their studies with their personal interests.", "How growing up in the same neighborhood influences people to develop similar interests.", "The different hobbies of five close friends and how each hobby reflects their individual personality.", "The importance of hobbies for university students living in Ho Chi Minh City."], answer: 2 }, { q: "According to the passage, what did Minh achieve in last year's photography competition?", options: ["He won first place in the category for landscape photography.", "He was selected to exhibit his work at a national photography festival.", "He received an honorable mention for a photograph taken at a local market.", "He won second place in the street photography category."], answer: 3 }, { q: "The word \"Her\" in paragraph 3 refers to _______.", options: ["The journal in which Lan records her thoughts about books.", "The online reading club that Lan participates in each week.", "Lan's personal library at home.", "The foreign literature that Lan has read in translation."], answer: 2 }, { q: "According to the passage, what first led Tuan to develop an interest in cooking?", options: ["He enrolled in a cooking class at the university and discovered he had natural talent.", "He was inspired by watching professional chefs on a television cooking competition.", "He wanted to impress his four friends by preparing meals from different countries for them.", "He spent several months at home during the COVID-19 pandemic and began experimenting with recipes."], answer: 3 }, { q: "According to the passage, what is Huong's goal for the coming year?", options: ["To begin coaching other university students who want to take up running.", "To run five days a week every week for a full year without missing a single session.", "To win a medal at a national half-marathon competition held in Ho Chi Minh City.", "To complete a full marathon at a race scheduled in Da Nang in November."], answer: 3 }, { q: "According to the passage, which of Bao's close friends has become equally enthusiastic about the board game Terraforming Mars ?", options: ["Huong.", "Minh.", "Lan.", "Tuan."], answer: 3 }, { q: "According to the passage, which of the following is NOT mentioned as something Lan does as part of her reading hobby?", options: ["Recommending books to her friends when asked.", "Keeping a journal with her thoughts about each book she finishes.", "Writing short book reviews that she publishes on a personal blog.", "Participating in an online reading club that meets each week."], answer: 2 }, { q: "The word \"pursuit\" in paragraph 4 is closest in meaning to _______.", options: ["A competitive sport that requires physical training and discipline.", "A temporary activity taken up out of curiosity or boredom.", "An activity that someone follows with serious effort and dedication.", "A professional skill that can eventually be turned into paid work."], answer: 2 }, { q: "The phrase \"physically active\" in paragraph 5 is closest in meaning to _______.", options: ["Always in good health and rarely affected by illness or injury.", "Preferring outdoor activities over hobbies that are done indoors.", "Regularly engaged in exercise and movement as part of daily life.", "Competitive in sports and always aiming to win against others."], answer: 2 }, { q: "What is the author's main purpose in writing this passage?", options: ["To explain how university students in Ho Chi Minh City typically spend their free time.", "To compare the hobbies of five friends and decide which one is the most valuable or meaningful.", "To argue that having a hobby is essential for maintaining good mental and physical health.", "To describe the unique hobbies and individual characters of five close friends in an engaging way."], answer: 3 }] },
    { title: "Hanoi: A City with One Thousand Years of History", paragraphs: ["Hanoi, the capital of Vietnam, is one of Southeast Asia's oldest and most historically layered cities. Its origins are traced back to 1010 CE, when Emperor Ly Thai To moved the Vietnamese imperial capital from Hoa Lu in Ninh Binh Province to a new site on the banks of the Red River — a location he chose because, according to historical accounts, he witnessed a golden dragon rising from the waters as his boat approached the shore. He named the new capital Thang Long, meaning \"ascending dragon,\" and it remained the political and cultural center of the Vietnamese nation for the next eight centuries. The city was renamed Hanoi — meaning \"between rivers\" or \"inside the rivers\" — in 1831 under Emperor Minh Mang of the Nguyen Dynasty.", "Throughout its long history, Hanoi has been shaped by the overlapping imprints of successive dynasties, colonial administrations, and modern development. The city's Old Quarter, known in Vietnamese as Phố Cổ , remains one of its most visited and historically significant neighborhoods. Originally organized in the eleventh century around craft guilds, each street in the Old Quarter was historically dedicated to a specific trade — silk merchants on one street, paper sellers on another, blacksmiths on a third — a system that gave rise to the neighborhood's distinctive character and that is still partially reflected in the street names today, many of which begin with the word Hàng , meaning \"merchandise\" or \"goods.\"", "The French colonial period, which began in earnest in the latter half of the nineteenth century and lasted until 1954, left a profound architectural legacy on Hanoi that remains visible in the city's streetscape today. The French administration transformed Hanoi into the administrative capital of French Indochina and undertook ambitious urban planning projects that introduced wide tree-lined boulevards, grand public buildings in neoclassical style, and the elegant villas of the French Quarter that still line the streets around Hoan Kiem Lake. The Hanoi Opera House, completed in 1911 and modeled on the Paris Opera, and the Long Bien Bridge, completed in 1902 and designed by the firm of Gustave Eiffel, are perhaps the two most recognizable surviving symbols of this era.", "Among Hanoi's most cherished cultural and religious landmarks is the Temple of Literature, known in Vietnamese as Văn Miếu . Built in 1070 during the reign of Emperor Ly Thanh Tong, the temple was originally dedicated to Confucius and served as the site of Vietnam's first university, the Imperial Academy, which was established in 1076 to educate the sons of the royal family and, later, talented young men from across the country. The temple's five courtyards, the serene pond, and the stone steles bearing the names and hometowns of doctoral graduates from 1442 onward are among the most evocative historical spaces in the city. Today, the Temple of Literature is a popular destination for students who come to pray for academic success before important examinations.", "Hoan Kiem Lake, situated at the heart of the old city, is perhaps Hanoi's most beloved public space and carries with it one of the city's best-known legends. According to tradition, the Vietnamese king Le Loi, who led the resistance against Chinese Ming Dynasty occupation in the early fifteenth century, was given a magical sword by a golden turtle living in the lake. With this sword, he drove out the invaders and restored Vietnamese independence. After the victory, as he was boating on the lake, a large turtle rose from the water and reclaimed the sword on behalf of the divine powers that had lent it. The lake was thereafter named Hoan Kiem, meaning \"Lake of the Returned Sword.\" Today, the lake is home to a critically endangered species of giant softshell turtle, making it a site of both cultural and biological significance.", "Modern Hanoi is a city of striking contrasts. Glass-fronted office towers and luxury hotels rise alongside ancient pagodas and colonial-era villas. Wide ring roads carry streams of motorcycles and cars past neighborhoods where traditional craft villages continue to produce lacquerware, silk, and handmade paper much as they have for centuries. The city's population has grown to over eight million people, and it continues to expand rapidly as Vietnam's economy develops and rural-to-urban migration accelerates. Despite the pace of change, Hanoi retains a distinctive character shaped by its history — a quality that visitors from abroad frequently describe as the city's most powerful and elusive appeal .", "In 2010, Hanoi celebrated the one-thousandth anniversary of its founding with a series of events that drew national and international attention and reinforced the city's sense of itself as the cultural and historical heart of Vietnam. For its residents and for Vietnamese people everywhere, Hanoi is not simply a place to live or work — it is a symbol of national continuity, endurance, and identity, carrying within its streets and lakes and temples a history that belongs to all Vietnamese people."], questions: [{ q: "What is the passage mainly about?", options: ["The impact of French colonial architecture on the modern appearance of Hanoi's city center.", "A guide to the best tourist attractions in Hanoi and how to visit them efficiently.", "A comparison of Hanoi with other capital cities in Southeast Asia in terms of historical significance.", "The history, cultural landmarks, and enduring identity of Hanoi across one thousand years."], answer: 3 }, { q: "According to the passage, what does the name \"Thang Long\" mean, and who gave the city this name?", options: ["\"Ascending dragon,\" named by Emperor Ly Thai To in 1010.", "\"Golden city,\" named by the first Nguyen Dynasty emperor in the early nineteenth century.", "\"City of turtles,\" named after the legend of the giant turtle in Hoan Kiem Lake.", "\"Between rivers,\" named by Emperor Minh Mang in 1831."], answer: 0 }, { q: "According to the passage, which of the following is NOT mentioned as something introduced to Hanoi during the French colonial period?", options: ["The elegant villas of the French Quarter around Hoan Kiem Lake.", "A network of underground railways connecting the city's major districts.", "Wide, tree-lined boulevards.", "Grand neoclassical public buildings."], answer: 1 }, { q: "According to the passage, what was the original purpose of the Temple of Literature when it was built in 1070?", options: ["It was constructed as a memorial to fallen soldiers of the Vietnamese resistance.", "It was designed as a public space where citizens could gather to discuss philosophy and literature.", "It was built as a royal palace for the emperor and his family during the Ly Dynasty.", "It was originally dedicated to Confucius and later became the site of Vietnam's first university."], answer: 3 }, { q: "According to the passage, why was Hoan Kiem Lake given its current name?", options: ["Because the lake sits between two rivers whose names together mean \"returned\" and \"sword\" in Vietnamese.", "Because a large turtle rose from the lake and reclaimed a magical sword after King Le Loi's victory.", "Because a golden dragon was seen rising from its waters when Emperor Ly Thai To arrived at the site.", "Because Emperor Minh Mang ordered the lake to be renamed in honor of a military victory in 1831."], answer: 1 }, { q: "According to the passage, in what year was the Hanoi Opera House completed?", options: ["1902.", "1911.", "1954.", "1976."], answer: 1 }, { q: "The word \"imprints\" in paragraph 2 is closest in meaning to _______.", options: ["Written documents that describe the city's development over time.", "Official records kept in government archives over many centuries.", "Marks or lasting influences left behind by past people or events.", "Physical boundaries drawn between different parts of the city."], answer: 2 }, { q: "The word \"dedicated\" in paragraph 4 refers to the fact that the Temple of Literature was originally _______.", options: ["Designed to store the stone steles recording the names of doctoral graduates.", "Built and given over in honor of Confucius as its primary purpose.", "Named after the founder of the Ly Dynasty who commissioned it.", "Opened to the general public as a place of education and debate."], answer: 1 }, { q: "The word \"appeal\" in paragraph 6 is closest in meaning to _______.", options: ["The quality of being attractive or interesting to people.", "A formal request made to an authority for a decision to be changed.", "A public announcement made to inform people about an important event.", "The ability of a city to grow quickly and attract investment."], answer: 0 }, { q: "What can be inferred from the passage about the significance of Hanoi's landmarks to Vietnamese people?", options: ["For Vietnamese people, Hanoi's sites carry deep historical and cultural meaning that connects them to a shared national identity.", "Vietnamese people regard Hanoi's landmarks mainly as architectural achievements rather than as cultural or spiritual symbols.", "The significance of Hanoi's landmarks is recognized more strongly by foreign visitors than by Vietnamese citizens themselves.", "Most of Hanoi's historical landmarks are valued primarily for their economic contribution to the tourism industry."], answer: 0 }] },
    { title: "Who Learns English More Effectively: Adults or Children?", paragraphs: ["The question of whether children or adults are more effective language learners is one of the most persistently debated topics in applied linguistics and language education. Popular opinion has long favored children, drawing on the everyday observation that young immigrants placed in a new country typically achieve native-like fluency within a few years while their adult counterparts frequently retain a noticeable foreign accent and grammatical imperfections for the rest of their lives. [1] This intuitive consensus has been reinforced by the influential Critical Period Hypothesis, first proposed by neurologist Wilder Penfield in the 1950s and later popularized by linguist Eric Lenneberg, which holds that the human brain's capacity for natural, effortless language acquisition is biologically constrained to a window that closes, at least partially, around the onset of puberty.", "The neurological basis for the critical period argument rests on evidence of age-related changes in brain plasticity. During early childhood, the brain is characterized by an extraordinary degree of neural flexibility, with new synaptic connections forming rapidly in response to linguistic input. As the brain matures, this plasticity progressively diminishes , and language learning shifts from a largely implicit, subconscious process — the kind that allows a three-year-old to absorb the grammatical structures of their mother tongue without formal instruction — to a more deliberate, rule-governed, and effortful cognitive activity. This shift, many researchers argue, explains why children appear to acquire the phonological system and grammar of a new language with far greater ease and naturalness than adults, who must consciously apply learned rules to produce utterances that fluent speakers generate automatically.", "What this standard account tends to overlook, however, is the considerable body of research demonstrating that adults consistently outperform children in the early and middle stages of second language learning, particularly in formal instructional settings. Studies conducted in the 1970s and 1980s by researchers including Roger Shuy, Stephen Krashen, and others found that adult learners typically acquire vocabulary, grammar rules, and reading and writing skills at a faster rate than child learners when the two groups are given equivalent amounts of instruction time. Adults bring to the learning process a range of cognitive advantages that children do not possess: a larger existing vocabulary in their first language that facilitates the learning of cognates and semantic relationships; stronger working memory and analytical reasoning skills; and the metacognitive ability to monitor their own learning, identify gaps, and deploy strategies deliberately. [2]", "The distinction between rate of acquisition and ultimate attainment is crucial to understanding the debate correctly. Adults may learn faster initially, but children who begin exposure to a second language before the age of roughly seven or eight show a strong tendency to achieve higher levels of ultimate attainment — particularly in pronunciation and in the intuitive application of grammatical patterns — than those who begin as teenagers or adults. This divergence in long-term outcomes has been attributed to both neurological factors and sociocultural ones: child learners have more time, more naturalistic exposure, and fewer inhibitions about making mistakes in front of peers, whereas adult learners are often constrained by professional time pressures, social anxiety about making errors, and the established identity of a competent first-language speaker that any adult language learner must, at least temporarily, surrender. [3]", "The role of motivation and context deserves particular attention in any honest assessment of this debate. An adult learner with strong intrinsic motivation, meaningful real-world exposure to the target language, and access to high-quality instruction can achieve a level of functional proficiency in English that far exceeds what many child learners accomplish in low-quality or low-input environments. Conversely, a child placed in a rich immersive environment — attending school, making friends, watching television, and playing in the target language — has access to a qualitatively different kind of input than a child learning English for two hours a week in a classroom, a distinction that is often elided in broad generalizations about childhood language learning advantages. [4]", "The most defensible conclusion that current research supports is not a simple ranking of one group over the other but a recognition that children and adults learn languages differently, bring different strengths and limitations to the task, and tend to excel in different dimensions of language competence. For educators and policy makers, this means that the optimal approach to English language teaching cannot be identical for both groups. Children benefit most from naturalistic, play-based, communicative approaches that maximize incidental exposure; adults benefit most from structured instruction that builds on their existing cognitive frameworks, provides explicit feedback, and connects language learning to meaningful personal and professional goals.", "The persistence of the popular belief that children are simply better language learners in every respect reflects not so much the weight of the evidence as the power of observable outcomes — the child immigrant who sounds like a native speaker, the adult who never loses their accent — that are vivid and emotionally compelling even when they are not representative of the full picture. Understanding the actual complexity of the relationship between age and language learning is not merely an academic exercise: it has direct implications for how societies design educational systems, allocate resources, and support the millions of adult migrants and refugees who need to acquire English or another language quickly and effectively to participate fully in the communities they have joined."], questions: [{ q: "Which of the following best describes the main argument of the passage?", options: ["Children are superior language learners in every respect, and education systems should therefore prioritize early childhood English instruction.", "Adults learn languages more effectively than children because of their stronger cognitive skills and greater motivation.", "Children and adults learn languages differently, with different strengths and in different dimensions, and teaching approaches should reflect this distinction.", "The Critical Period Hypothesis has been conclusively disproven and age has no significant effect on a person's ability to learn a second language."], answer: 2 }, { q: "According to the passage, who first proposed the Critical Period Hypothesis in the 1950s?", options: ["Wilder Penfield, a neurologist whose ideas were later popularized by Eric Lenneberg.", "Eric Lenneberg, a linguist who argued that language acquisition is biologically constrained.", "Roger Shuy, a researcher who studied the comparative learning rates of adults and children.", "Stephen Krashen, whose research in the 1970s and 1980s focused on adult language acquisition."], answer: 0 }, { q: "According to the passage, in which areas do adult learners typically outperform child learners in the early stages of second language learning?", options: ["The ability to acquire a native-like accent and to communicate naturally with fluent speakers.", "Pronunciation accuracy and the intuitive production of grammatically correct sentences.", "Immersive listening comprehension and the incidental absorption of idiomatic expressions.", "Vocabulary acquisition, grammar rules, and reading and writing skills under equivalent instruction time."], answer: 3 }, { q: "According to the passage, which of the following is NOT mentioned as a cognitive advantage that adult learners bring to language learning?", options: ["Greater emotional resilience that allows them to recover quickly from making mistakes.", "A larger first-language vocabulary that helps with learning related words in a new language.", "The ability to monitor their own learning and use deliberate strategies.", "Stronger working memory and analytical reasoning skills."], answer: 0 }, { q: "According to the passage, at approximately what age must a learner begin exposure to a second language to show a strong tendency toward higher ultimate attainment?", options: ["Before the age of five, when the brain's phonological processing is most flexible.", "Before puberty begins, typically between the ages of ten and twelve.", "Before the age of roughly seven or eight.", "Before the age of three, during the most critical window of neural development."], answer: 2 }, { q: "According to the passage, which teaching approach does the author recommend for adult English language learners?", options: ["Intensive immersion programs in which all instruction is delivered exclusively in the target language.", "Naturalistic, play-based, and communicative approaches that maximize incidental exposure.", "Child-focused methods adapted for adult use, since these have been shown to produce the fastest results.", "Structured instruction with explicit feedback that connects language learning to personal and professional goals."], answer: 3 }, { q: "The word \"diminishes\" in paragraph 2 is closest in meaning to _______.", options: ["Gradually becomes smaller or weaker over time.", "Shifts its primary function from one hemisphere of the brain to the other.", "Develops in unexpected ways that researchers have not yet been able to explain.", "Becomes more specialized and focused on a narrower range of functions."], answer: 0 }, { q: "The word \"constrained\" in paragraph 4 is closest in meaning to _______.", options: ["Frustrated by the slow pace of progress relative to their expectations.", "Motivated to work harder due to external pressure and competition.", "Encouraged to take more risks by a supportive learning environment.", "Limited or restricted by factors that prevent full freedom of action."], answer: 3 }, { q: "The phrase \"this divergence\" in paragraph 4 refers to _______.", options: ["The contrast between implicit and explicit language learning processes in the developing brain.", "The difference in long-term language attainment between learners who begin before age seven or eight and those who begin later.", "The difference between the Critical Period Hypothesis as proposed by Penfield and as later described by Lenneberg.", "The gap in learning rates between adults and children in formal classroom settings."], answer: 1 }, { q: "Which of the following square brackets [1] , [2] , [3] , or [4] best indicates where the following sentence could be added? 'Indeed, documented cases of adult learners achieving near-native proficiency in a second language, though statistically uncommon, demonstrate that the critical period is better understood as a probabilistic tendency than an absolute biological ceiling.'", options: ["[1]", "[2]", "[3]", "[4]"], answer: 1 }] },
    { title: "What Human Waste Can Teach Scientists", paragraphs: ["For most of human history, sewage has been regarded as little more than a public health problem to be managed, contained, and disposed of as efficiently as possible. The engineering triumphs of nineteenth-century sanitation — the construction of Joseph Bazalgette's London sewer system in the 1860s, the separation of drinking water from waste water that dramatically reduced mortality from cholera and typhoid — were celebrated as victories of hygiene over disease, not as the creation of a data resource. [1] Yet contemporary scientists have discovered that the aggregate biological output of a community, flowing unseen through its wastewater infrastructure, constitutes one of the most information-rich and democratically sampled datasets available for monitoring the health, behavior, and biochemistry of large human populations.", "The discipline that has formalized this insight is known as wastewater epidemiology or wastewater-based epidemiology (WBE), and its scientific foundations were established with unexpected speed and rigor in the early years of the twenty-first century. Its most dramatic proof of concept came with the COVID-19 pandemic, when researchers discovered that the SARS-CoV-2 virus shed in the feces of infected individuals — including asymptomatic carriers who had never sought medical testing — could be detected in municipal sewage at concentrations that accurately predicted community infection rates three to five days before clinical case numbers reflected the same trend. Several countries, including the Netherlands, Israel, and the United States, established national wastewater surveillance networks that became integral components of their pandemic response infrastructure, providing public health authorities with a real-time, population-level signal that clinical testing alone could not replicate.", "The applications of WBE extend far beyond infectious disease surveillance. Human urine and feces contain a biochemically promiscuous mixture of metabolites, hormones, pharmaceutical residues, dietary biomarkers, and genetic material that collectively encode a detailed portrait of the population that produced them. By analyzing the concentration of urinary biomarkers for opioids, benzodiazepines, and methamphetamine in wastewater, researchers have been able to construct detailed maps of illicit drug use across urban areas — information that is otherwise extremely difficult to obtain accurately because it depends on self-reporting by populations with strong incentives to conceal their behavior. Wastewater analysis has similarly been used to detect the presence of anabolic steroids during major sporting events, providing an independent verification method that complements but is distinct from the individual urine testing of athletes. [2]", "Nutritional surveillance represents another frontier for wastewater science. The metabolic byproducts of dietary intake — the breakdown products of vitamins, fiber, sugar, alcohol, and saturated fat — are excreted in patterns that can be analyzed to infer population-level dietary habits with a granularity that neither dietary surveys nor food sales data can achieve. A study conducted in several European cities found measurable differences in the wastewater profiles of communities with high versus low fiber consumption, correlating with independent epidemiological data on colorectal cancer rates and suggesting that WBE might eventually serve as an inexpensive and continuous monitoring tool for nutritionally linked non-communicable diseases. [3] Unlike self-reported dietary surveys, which are notorious for their susceptibility to social desirability bias — the tendency of respondents to report eating more healthfully than they actually do — wastewater data cannot lie.", "The ethical dimensions of wastewater surveillance are more complex than they might initially appear. Because wastewater data is collected at the level of the sewage catchment area rather than the individual, it has traditionally been considered to fall outside the frameworks of individual privacy protection that govern clinical and genomic data. This assumption has been progressively complicated by advances in sequencing technology: it is now theoretically possible, in small communities served by a single sewer outlet, to identify individual contributors to the wastewater stream through genomic analysis of the DNA present in the sample. As catchment areas become smaller and analytical tools become more precise, the line between anonymized population surveillance and identifiable individual monitoring becomes correspondingly thinner, raising questions about consent, data governance, and the appropriate limits of public health authority that the field has only begun to address. [4]", "The antibiotic resistance crisis has given wastewater science yet another urgent application. Sewage systems receive not only human biological waste but also the effluent of hospitals, pharmaceutical manufacturing facilities, and agricultural operations — all of which are major sources of antibiotic compounds and antibiotic-resistant bacteria. Monitoring wastewater for the presence and concentration of specific antibiotic resistance genes allows researchers to track the emergence and spread of resistant strains at a population level, identifying transmission hotspots and assessing the effectiveness of stewardship interventions in real time. Several research groups have argued that a global wastewater surveillance network, analogous in ambition to the flu surveillance systems that have operated since the mid-twentieth century, could provide the early warning infrastructure necessary to prevent the next major antibiotic resistance crisis from becoming an uncontrolled pandemic.", "What the science of wastewater ultimately demonstrates is that the boundary between what is considered waste and what is considered information is not fixed but contingent — dependent on the analytical tools available, the questions being asked, and the ethical frameworks within which the answers may be pursued. As those tools become more powerful and those questions more urgent, the humble sewer is being reimagined not merely as a conduit for disposal but as a continuous, city-scale biosensor whose outputs may, in the decades ahead, prove as valuable to public health as any clinical instrument yet devised."], questions: [{ q: "Which of the following best captures the central argument of the passage?", options: ["Wastewater-based epidemiology has proven so effective during the COVID-19 pandemic that clinical testing is no longer a necessary component of disease surveillance.", "The primary obstacle to expanding wastewater surveillance globally is the cost of the analytical technology required to process large volumes of sewage samples.", "Human wastewater contains a wealth of biological information that can be analyzed to monitor population health, behavior, and disease — though this potential raises ethical questions that remain incompletely resolved.", "The construction of modern sewage systems in the nineteenth century was the most important public health achievement in human history."], answer: 2 }, { q: "According to the passage, how many days in advance did wastewater surveillance predict COVID-19 community infection rates before clinical case numbers reflected the same trend?", options: ["Three to five days.", "Seven to ten days.", "Up to two weeks before clinical testing confirmed the rise.", "One to two days."], answer: 0 }, { q: "According to the passage, which of the following is NOT mentioned as an application of wastewater-based epidemiology?", options: ["Inferring population-level dietary habits from metabolic byproducts.", "Tracking the spread of vector-borne diseases carried by mosquitoes.", "Monitoring antibiotic resistance gene concentrations in sewage.", "Detecting illicit drug use patterns across urban areas."], answer: 1 }, { q: "According to the passage, why is wastewater data considered more reliable than self-reported dietary surveys for nutritional surveillance?", options: ["Wastewater samples can be collected continuously and automatically without requiring cooperation from the population being studied.", "Unlike surveys, wastewater data cannot be distorted by the tendency of respondents to report eating more healthfully than they actually do.", "Wastewater analysis is significantly less expensive to conduct than large-scale dietary survey programs.", "Wastewater profiles can distinguish between dietary habits in different demographic groups within the same community."], answer: 1 }, { q: "According to the passage, which sources contribute antibiotic compounds and resistant bacteria to sewage systems?", options: ["Industrial food processing plants and municipal composting facilities.", "Urban runoff from roads and parks treated with pesticides and herbicides.", "Hospitals, pharmaceutical manufacturing facilities, and agricultural operations.", "Households, schools, and public swimming pools that use chlorinated water."], answer: 2 }, { q: "The word \"aggregate\" in paragraph 1 is closest in meaning to _______.", options: ["Carefully selected and filtered to remove irrelevant components.", "Unprocessed and in its most natural, unfiltered state.", "Generated over a very long period of time by successive populations.", "Collected together as a combined total from many individual sources."], answer: 3 }, { q: "The word \"promiscuous\" in paragraph 3 is closest in meaning to _______.", options: ["Unstable over time and therefore difficult to preserve for laboratory analysis.", "Highly toxic and potentially dangerous to the researchers who handle it.", "Indiscriminately mixed and containing a wide variety of different elements.", "Produced in greater quantities than the body actually requires for normal function."], answer: 2 }, { q: "The word \"complicated\" in paragraph 5 refers to the fact that advances in sequencing technology have _______.", options: ["Slowed the adoption of WBE by introducing new regulatory requirements for laboratory certification.", "Created new disagreements among public health officials about the value of WBE.", "Made wastewater analysis significantly more expensive and technically demanding.", "Made the assumption that wastewater data is inherently anonymous less straightforward than previously believed."], answer: 3 }, { q: "What can be inferred about the author's overall attitude toward wastewater-based epidemiology?", options: ["The author regards WBE as a genuinely transformative scientific development whose potential is considerable but whose ethical and governance challenges must be taken seriously.", "The author is indifferent to the scientific applications of WBE and uses the topic primarily as a vehicle for discussing data privacy and public health ethics.", "The author believes that wastewater surveillance has already fully demonstrated its value during COVID-19 and that its expansion should proceed without further debate.", "The author is primarily skeptical, focusing on the ethical risks of surveillance and arguing that WBE should be subject to strict limitations."], answer: 0 }, { q: "Which of the following square brackets [1] , [2] , [3] , or [4] best indicates where the following sentence could be added? 'The prospect of a global wastewater atlas capable of tracking the real-time spread of resistant strains across national borders has moved from theoretical speculation to active research agenda within the past decade.'", options: ["[1]", "[2]", "[3]", "[4]"], answer: 3 }] },
  ] },
];
